Four Foundation classes the Swift overlay imports and Foundation never defined.

libswiftFoundation loads in every Swift application and lists 54 ObjC classes as undefined imports.
Fifty of them Foundation defines. These four it did not, measured with llvm-nm against the built
framework and with NSDateInterval and NSMeasurement as the control, both of which come back
defined by the same command:

  NSPersonNameComponents          0
  NSOrderedCollectionChange       0
  NSOrderedCollectionDifference   0
  NSURLSessionWebSocketMessage    0
  NSDateInterval                  1      <- control
  NSMeasurement                   1      <- control

They are written out rather than stubbed. All four are plain value objects with no behaviour to
guess at, and a stub whose accessors answer nil is a WRONG answer rather than a missing one, which
is harder to find later.

NSOrderedCollectionDifference keeps removals and insertions concatenated in that order, because
that is the order the changes must be APPLIED in and so the order fast enumeration must yield.

A HEADER IS NOT REACHABLE JUST BY EXISTING. Foundation's own include directory is globbed, but a
<Foundation/...> include resolves through buck/generated/sdk_framework_buck_src.bzl, an EXPLICIT
generated map. Without an entry there the compile fails with "file not found" for a header sitting
in the right directory, and adding the imports to Foundation.h would have broken every consumer.
The generator, cider-sdk-header-roots --framework-roots, reads src/darwin/framework-include, which
is NOT present in a materialised working copy, so the four entries are added by hand.
scripts/checks/buck-pin-paths-check.nu validates that every mapped path exists and reports
sdk_paths 3578 paths, 0 missing.

